WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.330
Interviewer: What is the biggest challenge
in transitioning from your physics career

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.720
to your science educational efforts?

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.430
Quinn: Well, I guess the biggest challenge
was giving up my physics career.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:15.140
I was about to retire, and I wanted to retire
for various reasons.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.280
But most physicists go on doing physics
research, particularly theorists like me who

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.930
don't need any equipment to do it.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:29.440
However, I was committing myself to a project
that would take a year and a half, full-time

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.969
work, to chair the committee that developed
the framework for K-12 science education.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:41.059
And it was a choice: Do I take this one on
or do I continue doing my physics?

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:45.969
And I thought, well I could write a few more
physics papers that would be read by a few

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:52.429
people, but if we really could do something
that improved science education nationally,

00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:53.899
that would be a worthwhile effort.

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:57.959
So I decided to stop doing physics and start
doing education work.

